  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>How Ohio Hourly Paychecks Are Calculated in 2026</h2>
    <p>
      Ohio payroll involves up to <strong>five separate layers of tax withholding</strong>: Federal income tax, FICA (Social Security and Medicare), Ohio state income tax, Ohio municipal (city/village) income tax, and—depending on where you live—an Ohio School District Income Tax (SDIT).
    </p>
    <div class="ac-formula">
Weekly Gross ($)        = (Regular Hours × Hourly Rate) + (Overtime Hours × Hourly Rate × 1.5)
Annual Gross ($)        = Weekly Gross × 52
Paycheck Gross ($)      = Annual Gross ÷ Pay Periods (52, 26, or 24)

Federal Taxable ($)     = max(0, Annual Gross − Annual 401(k) − Annual Sec. 125 Health − 2026 Std. Deduction)
Ohio Taxable ($)        = max(0, Annual Gross − Annual 401(k) − Annual Sec. 125 Health − OH Personal Exemption)
Ohio State Tax (2026)   = $0 if Ohio Taxable ≤ $26,050; else (Ohio Taxable − $26,050) × 0.0275

Ohio Municipal Tax ($)  = (Paycheck Gross − Sec. 125 Health) × Municipal Rate%
FICA Tax ($)            = (FICA Wages × 0.062 up to $176,100 cap) + (FICA Wages × 0.0145)
    </div>
    <h3>Critical Ohio Payroll Rules Most National Calculators Miss</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2026 Ohio Flat 2.75% Rate (HB 96):</strong> Ohio completed its multi-year rate reduction in 2026. The first <strong>$26,050</strong> of Ohio taxable income is taxed at <strong>0%</strong>, and every dollar above $26,050 is taxed at a flat <strong>2.75%</strong> (down from the old 3.5% top bracket).</li>
      <li><strong>401(k) Contributions Are Taxed by Ohio Cities:</strong> Under Ohio Revised Code Chapter 718, employee elective 401(k) and 403(b) deferrals reduce Federal and Ohio state income tax, <em>but remain subject to Ohio municipal income tax</em> (such as Columbus 2.5%, Cleveland 2.5%, and Cincinnati 1.8%).</li>
      <li><strong>Work City vs. Home City (RITA / CCA):</strong> Your employer withholds municipal tax for the city where you physically work. If you live in a different Ohio municipality, you may owe a small balance to your home city via RITA or CCA if it does not grant a 100% credit for workplace taxes paid.</li>
      <li><strong>Ohio Personal Exemptions:</strong> Ohio grants tiered personal exemptions ($2,400 for MAGI ≤ $40,000; $2,150 for $40,001–$80,000; $1,900 above $80,000), which slightly lowers actual state income tax compared to raw gross wages.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

