  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Ohio Hourly Paychecks</h2>
    <div class="ac-faq-list">
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>1. What is the Ohio state income tax rate in 2026?</h3>
        <p>Under Ohio HB 96, tax year 2026 uses a simplified two-tier flat structure: <strong>0% on Ohio taxable income up to $26,050</strong>, and a flat <strong>2.75% on all income above $26,050</strong>. There is no longer a 3.5% top bracket.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>2. What are the 2026 municipal income tax rates in major Ohio cities?</h3>
        <p>Major Ohio city rates are <strong>2.5%</strong> in Columbus, Cleveland, Akron, Toledo, and Dayton; <strong>1.8%</strong> in Cincinnati; and <strong>2.75%</strong> in Youngstown. Unincorporated Ohio townships levy 0% municipal income tax.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>3. Why is Ohio city tax still deducted on my 401(k) contribution?</h3>
        <p>Ohio municipal tax law follows Medicare-wage rules rather than federal income tax rules for retirement deferrals. While your traditional 401(k) lowers Federal and Ohio state withholding, cities like Columbus, Cleveland, and Cincinnati still tax your gross wages before 401(k) deductions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>4. What is the Ohio School District Income Tax (SDIT)?</h3>
        <p>Roughly one-third of Ohio's 600+ school districts levy a voter-approved local income tax (typically 0.5% to 2.0%) based on where you <strong>live</strong>, not where you work. Major metro districts (Columbus City, Cleveland Municipal, Cincinnati Public) do not levy SDIT, while many suburban and rural districts do.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>5. How much will I take home on $25 an hour in Ohio?</h3>
        <p>At $25/hr for 40 hours per week ($52,000/year), a Single worker in Columbus or Cleveland (2.5% city tax) with no pre-tax benefit deductions takes home roughly <strong>$1,615 bi-weekly</strong> (~$41,990/year). In a 0% township, take-home rises to about <strong>$1,665 bi-weekly</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="ac-disclaimer">
      Disclaimer: Estimates are for paycheck budgeting based on 2026 IRS tax tables and Ohio HB 96 state rates. Actual employer withholding varies with W-4 Step 2–4 adjustments, Ohio IT-4 exemptions, and local RITA/CCA municipal residency credits. Not tax or payroll advice.
    </p>
  </section>

